  "id": "Compound::DB01208 - Gene::1244",
  "verbalization": "Sparfloxacin, a quinolone antibiotic, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Candiduria and Cerebral Thrombosis, both of which are associated with Lomefloxacin, a closely related quinolone. The evidence suggests that Lomefloxacin binds to Abcc2, a gene involved in drug efflux, which may contribute to the pathophysiology of these conditions. The shared ancestry of Sparfloxacin and Lomefloxacin within the quinolone class indicates a potential mechanistic link between these compounds and their interaction with Abcc2. The similarity between Sparfloxacin and Besifloxacin, which also binds to Abcc2, further supports the notion that quinolones may exert their effects through a common mechanism involving the Abcc2 transporter. The final scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the evidence is not entirely conclusive. The integration of these findings implies that quinolone antibiotics may modulate Abcc2 activity, potentially influencing drug resistance or toxicity, but the exact role of Abcc2 in these processes remains to be fully elucidated.",
